    <header class="page-head">
      <div class="kicker">Research note · DRAFT · 2026-08-0X</div>
      <h1>[TITLE — John's pick]</h1>
      <p class="standfirst">
        Five companies have signed $2.6 trillion of forward obligations —
        last quarter's additions equate to $405 million an hour, every
        day, for 91 days. A careful attempt to read the number, against
        every capital program that came before it.
      </p>
    </header>

    <p>
      Between the end of March and the end of June 2026, five companies —
      Meta, Alphabet, Microsoft, Oracle, and Amazon — added roughly
      <strong>$885&nbsp;billion</strong> of new forward obligations to their
      financial statements: leases signed on data centers that do not yet
      exist, and purchase commitments for capacity, hardware, and power not
      yet delivered.<sup>1</sup>
    </p>
    <p>
      That equates to about <strong>$9.7&nbsp;billion of new signing per
      day — every day, Monday through Sunday, for 91 days</strong>.
      Roughly $405&nbsp;million an hour.
    </p>

    <figure class="chart">
      <img src="/img/research/scale/fig1_pace_nest.png" width="1680" height="945"
           alt="Three nested-square panels showing one hour of signing inside one day, one day inside the quarter, and the quarter inside all signed obligations, each at true area ratio." />
    </figure>

    <p>
      Those signatures joined a book of forward obligations that now
      stands at <strong>$2.61&nbsp;trillion</strong>: $1,090.5&nbsp;billion of leases
      signed but not yet commenced, and $1,516.1&nbsp;billion of purchase
      obligations — commitments executed ahead of delivery, stated as
      undiscounted future payments.<sup>2</sup> Behind it sits capital
      already deployed: $494.3&nbsp;billion of debt at face value and
      $285.5&nbsp;billion of lease liabilities on balance sheet — a full
      disclosed stack of $3.39&nbsp;trillion, though those two layers are
      funded and running, not forward, and this note keeps them separate.
      Their customers, in turn, owe the five $2,337.5&nbsp;billion of
      contracted future revenue — remaining performance obligations
      (RPO), in the accounting term.
    </p>
    <p>
      Three different numbers describe this deployment, and they should not be
      blurred. What the five <strong>spend</strong> each year on capital —
      roughly $570&nbsp;billion over their latest reported twelve months,
      about 1.7 percent of United States gross domestic product (GDP) — is a
      level American infrastructure has reached before. What they have
      <strong>signed</strong> and not yet taken delivery of — the
      $2.61&nbsp;trillion — has no counterpart at this scale in any private
      ledger we can find. And the <strong>pace</strong> of the
      signing is accelerating. This note is an attempt to read all three
      numbers carefully: what they include, what they exclude, and where, if
      anywhere, history offers a ruler.
    </p>

    <h2>1 · The Stack, as Disclosed</h2>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="data">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th></th>
            <th>Leases signed,<br/>not commenced<br/><span class="basis">undiscounted</span></th>
            <th>Purchase<br/>obligations<br/><span class="basis">as labeled</span></th>
            <th>Debt<br/><span class="basis">face</span></th>
            <th>Balance-sheet<br/>leases<br/><span class="basis">present value</span></th>
            <th>RPO<br/><span class="basis">as disclosed</span></th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {stack.map((r) => (
            <tr class={r.co === 'Total' ? 'total' : ''}>
              <td>{r.co}</td>
              <td class="num">{r.lnyc}</td>
              <td class="num">{r.po}</td>
              <td class="num">{r.debt}</td>
              <td class="num">{r.bsl}</td>
              <td class="num">{r.rpo}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="table-note">
        $ billions, from each company's most recent filing (accessions in the
        footnotes). Categories are as each issuer labels them and are not one
        economic quantity; a present-value-consistent total would be smaller,
        and the screen excludes disclosed contingent items — a conservative
        screen in the other direction.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="chart">
      <img src="/img/research/scale/fig3_accretion.png" width="1680" height="945"
           alt="Paired bars for March and June 2026 showing one quarter's growth: leases not yet commenced up $269.1 billion, purchase obligations up $616.0 billion, RPO up $234.9 billion, across the four calendar-quarter filers." />
    </figure>

    <p>
      The June quarter's growth was verified line by line against the
      filings. Alphabet's purchase obligations rose from $332.4&nbsp;billion
      to $811.0&nbsp;billion in the quarter — the largest single move — under
      disclosure language that is word-for-word identical in both quarters;
      the increase sits almost entirely in contracts with terms beyond one
      year, which the company attributes to "long-term supply agreements to
      secure future production capacity for technical
      infrastructure."<sup>4</sup> Microsoft's not-yet-commenced leases rose
      from $196.6&nbsp;billion to $329.1&nbsp;billion — and its new annual
      report adds, for the first time, that some of these arrangements are
      "subject to certain contractual conditions being met." The
      conditionality entered the disclosure in the same quarter the number
      doubled.<sup>5</sup> Meta's figure excludes roughly $68&nbsp;billion of
      further data-center leases signed in July, after its quarter closed —
      the quarter's true signing pace ran ahead of the number above.<sup>3</sup>
    </p>
    <p>
      How unusual is a footnote stack of this size? For every other large
      balance sheet in America, the answer is: there is nothing like it. The
      cleanest comparison is the one line that means the same thing in every
      filing — leases signed but not yet commenced, a standard disclosure
      under the current lease-accounting rules (ASC&nbsp;842). The five
      compute five carry <strong>$1,090.5&nbsp;billion</strong> on that
      line. AT&amp;T, Verizon, Apple, Walmart, ExxonMobil, Boeing, NextEra,
      Duke Energy, Toyota, and Volkswagen — ten of the largest obligor
      balance sheets in the world — carry, combined, <strong>less than
      $7&nbsp;billion</strong>.<sup>6</sup> For the traditional heavyweights,
      the footnotes are a rounding error on the debt: AT&amp;T's
      $136&nbsp;billion of borrowings dwarf its $25&nbsp;billion of purchase
      obligations. For the compute five, the debt is the rounding error — the
      forward commitments in the footnotes run roughly three times the
      on-balance-sheet stack. The single partial exception among the
      comparators is fitting: ExxonMobil's $54&nbsp;billion of take-or-pay
      and unconditional purchase commitments exceed its $44&nbsp;billion of
      debt — take-or-pay being, as the next section recounts, an
      energy-industry invention.
    </p>
    <p>
      A word on the perimeter, because it is drawn deliberately. Five
      companies is not the whole deployment; it is its most conservative,
      most verifiable core. One tier down sit the specialist GPU operators —
      CoreWeave, Nebius, and their peers — whose own leases and purchase
      obligations are real additional paper, but whose revenue backlogs are
      substantially the same dollars that appear above as the five's
      purchase obligations: one commitment, counted at two links of the
      chain. Summing the chain without netting it would inflate the total,
      so we do not. And outside the count entirely sit the private
      laboratories — including the single most-quoted figure in this
      discourse, OpenAI's reported $1.4&nbsp;trillion of compute agreements
      [VERIFY: press cite at ship] — which appear here only to the extent
      they have already landed inside a filer's disclosed backlog. Every
      number in this note is, by construction, an undercount.
    </p>

    <p>
      Three more rulers, then history — the first two in the same units,
      dollars of obligation. Against their own borrowings: the
      $2.61&nbsp;trillion of forward signatures is more than five times
      everything the five companies have themselves borrowed, at face.
      Against the heavyweights': it is roughly three and a half times the
      combined total debt of all eight American comparator companies
      above — AT&amp;T, Verizon, Apple, Walmart, ExxonMobil, NextEra,
      Duke, and Boeing together.<sup>6</sup> And against their own money:
      it equals roughly five years of the five firms' combined operating
      cash flow, spoken for in advance.
      Against everyone else's: it exceeds the entire annual economic output
      of Italy — on the International Monetary Fund's current figures,
      only seven countries produce more in a year than these five companies
      have signed forward.<sup>20</sup> Until roughly three years ago,
      these were famously the least indebted large companies in the world.
    </p>

    <h2>2 · The Precedents</h2>

    <figure class="photo">
      <img src="/img/research/scale/photo_promontory_1869.jpg" width="1600" height="1218"
           alt="East and West shaking hands at the laying of the last rail at Promontory Summit, 1869." />
      <figcaption>
        East and West shaking hands at the laying of the last rail,
        Promontory Summit, Utah, May 10, 1869. Photograph by Andrew J.
        Russell. (Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.)
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="chart">
      <img src="/img/research/scale/fig2_ranked_share.png" width="1680" height="945"
           alt="Ranked horizontal bars of peak construction spending as a share of GDP: UK Railway Mania about 8 percent, US railroads 1880s derived band, AI compute 2026 solid 1.7 percent with guided extension to about 2.5 percent, US railroads 1854, interstate construction, telecom, electrification, canals." />
    </figure>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="data">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Program</th><th>Share of GDP</th><th>Duration at share</th><th>Financing</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {precedents.map((r) => (
            <tr class={r.build.startsWith('AI compute') ? 'total' : ''}>
              <td>{r.build}</td><td>{r.peak}</td>
              <td>{r.dur}</td><td>{r.fin}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      America has built at this share of its economy before. United States
      railroad investment peaked at about <strong>2.6 percent of GDP in
      1854</strong> and ran near two percent for much of that decade; in the
      1880s the railroads absorbed 15 to 20 percent of all national
      investment (the conversion to a share of gross national product,
      roughly 2.5 to 4 percent, is a derivation from scholarly estimates
      rather than a published figure).<sup>7</sup> Britain's Railway Mania
      remains the all-time record: railway investment reached roughly
      <strong>eight percent of British GDP in 1847</strong> — about half of
      all domestic investment in the country — for a single extraordinary
      year.<sup>8</sup> Electrification ran on the order of one percent of
      gross national product (GNP) through the 1920s. The Interstate Highway
      System put about <strong>1.3 percent of GDP into construction,
      sustained for fifteen years</strong> — the durability record. The
      telecom and fiber program of 1996–2001 peaked at <strong>1.10 to 1.15
      percent of GDP, for two years</strong>, with sector capital spending
      growing 28 percent a year against 10 percent revenue
      growth.<sup>9</sup> The canal era peaked below one percent.
    </p>
    <p>
      Today's deployment: the five companies' latest reported twelve months of
      capital spending sum to roughly $570&nbsp;billion — with Amazon's
      $173.0&nbsp;billion taken from the printed trailing-twelve-month column
      of its own cash-flow statement — against a $32.5&nbsp;trillion economy:
      <strong>1.7 percent of GDP, almost exactly the railroad decade's
      average share</strong> (the 1850s ran about 1.7 percent), below the
      single-year peaks of 1854 and 1847. And the companies' own published
      guidance for 2026 sums to roughly $800&nbsp;billion — about 2.5 percent
      of GDP, a share above any single American year in the table. That
      figure is computed from the disclosed guides, which mix fiscal years
      and lease treatments; it is the companies' stated intent, not our
      projection.<sup>21</sup> On spending alone, this is what the biggest
      capital programs in history looked like — run, so far, at their average pace,
      with their peak pace guided. That is the familiar part.
    </p>
    <p>
      What the record does not contain is the signing. The historical programs were
      overwhelmingly <strong>pay-as-built</strong>: railroads sold
      construction bonds and spent the proceeds as track was laid; the
      interstates were appropriated year by year; even the fiber glut was
      largely spent before it was owed. The 2026 deployment inverts the sequence —
      decade-scale obligations are executed in advance of construction, which
      concentrates the commitment into a stock that can be measured and
      compared. Forward-signed capacity obligations do have a history, and it
      is instructive precisely because of its size. In the natural-gas
      industry of the 1970s and 1980s, pipelines signed take-or-pay contracts
      — pay for the gas whether or not you take it — during a shortage, to
      secure supply when access mattered more than price. When demand and
      prices turned, the pipelines' recorded take-or-pay liabilities reached
      <strong>$11.7&nbsp;billion</strong> by the end of 1986; roughly
      $10&nbsp;billion of obligations ultimately crystallized, of which
      pipelines absorbed about $4&nbsp;billion — and in a single 1986 round,
      $5.4&nbsp;billion of liabilities were extinguished for payments of
      $700&nbsp;million, about thirteen cents on the dollar. The episode
      required four orders of the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission (FERC)
      and the better part of a decade to unwind.<sup>10</sup>
    </p>
    <p>
      In Washington State, the Washington Public Power Supply System (WPPSS)
      financed five nuclear plants on "hell or high water" participant
      contracts — obligations owed whether or not the plants were ever
      finished. Electricity demand grew at a third of the forecast rate;
      construction on two plants stopped; and in 1983 the Washington Supreme
      Court held that the participants had lacked authority to sign, voiding
      the contracts and producing what was then the largest municipal bond
      default in American history: $2.25&nbsp;billion of principal on which
      holders recovered about thirty cents on the dollar.<sup>11</sup>
    </p>
    <p>
      Those are the closest structural precedents, and their size is the
      point: the WPPSS program, in full, amounted to about <strong>0.65
      percent of a single year's GDP in its day</strong>. The gas industry's recorded
      take-or-pay liabilities amounted to roughly a quarter of one percent.
      The compute five's signed-forward stock is approximately
      <strong>eight percent of a single year's GDP</strong> — an
      obligation stock set against one year's output, stated for scale. The only forward book of comparable scale in the
      American ledger is the federal government's own undelivered orders —
      $1,994&nbsp;billion at the end of fiscal 2024, about 6.9 percent of
      that year's GDP — and the payer there is the sovereign.<sup>12</sup>
      At the June quarter's pace, the five companies sign obligations equal
      to that entire WPPSS program roughly every five and a half hours, and
      the crystallized take-or-pay episode about once a day — comparisons of
      a signing rate to realized historical losses, offered for scale rather
      than symmetry.<sup>13</sup>
    </p>
    <p>
      One honest objection belongs here: housing is bigger. Residential
      investment peaked at 6.7 percent of GDP in 2005, and home mortgage debt
      across all holders — $14.8&nbsp;trillion today — peaked near 77 percent
      of GDP in early 2008; commercial real-estate debt crossed
      $5&nbsp;trillion this spring.<sup>22</sup> These are multiples of
      anything in this note. The distinction is not that mortgage risk was
      dispersed — 2008 settled what dispersion is worth when a single
      national factor moves, and the losses landed on leveraged holders
      either way. The distinction is the collateral and the obligor. A
      house is a seventy-five-year asset with intrinsic demand; the
      hardware under these commitments depreciates in five or six years,
      in a market where the next silicon generation reprices the last one.
      And where mortgage debt is owed by tens of millions of households,
      this stack is owed by five companies — five underwriting decisions
      and five disclosure regimes.
      Housing's true lesson runs the other way, and we return to it at
      the close: the largest asset class in the economy is also the most
      measured — appraisal standards, price indices, securitization
      surveillance — because size left no alternative. In the American
      economy, only housing, commercial real estate, the cumulative energy
      system, and the federal government itself have reached the scale this
      stack now occupies. Each of them took fifty to a hundred years to get
      there. This took about three.
    </p>

    <h2>3 · What Landed, and What Was Paid</h2>

    <figure class="photo">
      <img src="/img/research/scale/photo_satsop.jpg" width="1600" height="2133"
           alt="The unfinished cooling towers of the abandoned Satsop nuclear plant in Washington State." />
      <figcaption>
        The unfinished Satsop cooling towers, Washington — abandoned after
        the WPPSS default. (CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.)
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Every earlier program at this scale stressed the capital structure that
      financed it, and the record of what followed is worth stating plainly,
      because it is a record about paper, not about technology. The railroads
      were transformative and the rail bonds defaulted anyway: by the
      mid-1870s roughly <strong>40 percent of American railroad bonds were in
      default</strong>, and more than 18 percent of the nation's mileage sat
      in receivers' hands by 1877; the panic of 1893 put 132 railroads and
      27,570 miles into receivership in a single year, the receivership share
      peaked just above twenty percent in 1894, and over the quarter-century
      to 1898 a third of the country's mileage passed through
      receivership.<sup>14</sup> Freight rates fell by more than half, in
      nominal terms, across the last third of the century — through the booms
      as well as the panics.<sup>15</sup> The canal era ended with
      <strong>eight states and the Territory of Florida in default</strong>
      in 1841–42.<sup>16</sup> The utility pyramid of the 1920s ended with 53
      holding companies in bankruptcy or receivership between 1929 and
      1936.<sup>17</sup> The fiber program ended with a trillion dollars of
      sector debt — the figure is the FCC chairman's, in Senate testimony —
      some 24 of the 30 largest carriers in bankruptcy within three years,
      and telecom bond recoveries averaging sixteen cents.<sup>18</sup> In
      every case the capacity was eventually used — Google was buying the
      distressed fiber by 2005. And in every case, the obligations that
      financed it settled well below face value.
    </p>
    <p>
      That distinction — between the capacity and the obligations that
      financed it — is why the composition of today's stack deserves more
      attention than its total. The
      $3.39&nbsp;trillion is not one credit quality. It spans a spectrum that
      the precedents teach us to read: at one end, unconditional
      hell-or-high-water obligations (WPPSS's kind — which, under enough
      stress, a court voided anyway); then take-or-pay with regulatory or
      renegotiation outs (the gas kind, settled at a deep discount to face);
      then cancellable and deferrable commitments (the aircraft-backlog
      kind); and at the far end, obligations of the sovereign. The filings
      themselves hint at where their entries sit. Microsoft now notes that a
      portion of its $329&nbsp;billion in unopened leases is "subject to
      certain contractual conditions being met." Meta labels its
      residual-value guarantees "not probable" of payment and holds a further
      $14.7&nbsp;billion of capacity explicitly contingent. Amazon's newest
      customer commitments — $100&nbsp;billion from one counterparty over
      eight years, more than $100&nbsp;billion from another over ten — are
      disclosed as including obligations "tied to AWS chip performance":
      backlog whose face value depends on the silicon
      performing.<sup>19</sup> We publish these classifications as the
      companies state them; we grade nothing. The historical record is
      consistent on one point: in every precedent, the composition of the
      obligations — which were unconditional, which were contingent —
      was examined closely only after the cycle turned.
    </p>

    <h2>4 · Close</h2>
    <p>
      Capital at this scale has always been followed by standardized
      measurement — Henry Varnum Poor's railroad manual in 1868, the
      Chicago Board of Trade's grain grades in 1856, Platts's oil-price
      assessments in 1923 — typically within two decades of the program.
      This deployment compresses that timeline, because capital committed
      by forward contract needs its reference data at signing, not
      after: credit agreements in the GPU economy already reference
      market lease rates, as the Financial Times reported of one
      $2.6&nbsp;billion facility in July. And no single instrument covers
      it. Obligations of this size, on assets this short-lived, call for
      market rates on the pricing side, utilization and realized-revenue
      measures on the efficacy side, hardware values on the collateral
      side, and classification of the obligations themselves on the
      credit side. The numbers above are as filed. Rates were published
      in the rail booms and in the rail receiverships alike.
    </p>

