        <li>Aggregate revenue per ton-mile, Poor's/ICC series via AAR and NBER/FRED; general deflation 1873–96 means the real decline is smaller.</li>
        <li>Wallis, Sylla &amp; Grinath, NBER WP 10753 (2004).</li>
        <li>SEC-derived count via Living New Deal (PUHCA); 53 holding companies, $1.7B in securities, 1929–36.</li>
        <li>Powell, written Senate Commerce testimony, July 30, 2002 (~$1T sector debt); Oesterle (2003) (24 of 30); Altman &amp; Fanjul, NYU Salomon (2003) (2002 telecom recoveries ~16¢).</li>
        <li>Amazon Q2 2026 10-Q, RPO discussion (OpenAI +$100.0B/8yr; Anthropic +&gt;$100.0B/10yr; "tied to AWS chip performance").</li>
        <li>IMF World Economic Outlook, April 2026, nominal GDP (2025: France $3,369B vs. the $3,386B stack; six countries larger).</li>
        <li>Capex: Microsoft FY26 $115.95B (incl. finance leases); Alphabet TTM $132.5B (sum of disclosed quarters); Meta TTM $92.4B (incl. finance-lease principal); Amazon TTM $173.0B (printed TTM column, Q2 2026 10-Q cash-flow statement); Oracle FY26 $55.7B. Bases mixed as noted. GDP: BEA 2026 Q2 advance, $32,475.2B SAAR. Guidance: Alphabet $195–205B; Amazon ~$220B cash capex (Q2 call); Meta $130–145B; Microsoft FY27 ~$175B — reflecting a 15-to-25-year useful-life change and lease reclassification, with calendar-2026 expectations stated as unchanged; Oracle FY27 ~$70B net.</li>
        <li>FRED A011RE1Q156NBEA (residential investment share); Fed Z.1 all-sectors home mortgages ($14.818T, Q1 2026; $11.276T at the 2008 Q1 peak ≈ 77% of GDP); MBA (June 2026) commercial/multifamily $5.02T. Shares computed from the disclosed figures.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="method-note">
        Method: the focal figure ($2.61T “signed forward”) sums the two
        undiscounted forward categories only — leases not yet commenced
        and purchase obligations. The full disclosed stack ($3.39T) adds
        debt at face and balance-sheet leases at present value, mixes
        measurement bases, and appears only with its components labeled. A
        present-value-consistent total would be smaller; contingent items
        are excluded. GDP comparisons follow one convention: annual
        spending is set against the same year's GDP (flow against flow);
        obligation stocks are set against a single year's GDP for scale
        only, and are labeled as such wherever they appear. Windows are labeled
        throughout; deflated figures use the BLS CPI-U, figure-year annual
        average, per the method stated in our Itel note. Pre-1929 GNP
        denominators are scholarly reconstructions carrying roughly ±10%
        uncertainty. This note is descriptive; it forecasts nothing.
      </p>
